CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ reference to function: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ pointer to function: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.spec.partial.match]/1, [temp.deduct.type]/10,
[temp.spec.partial.order]: `R (&)(A...)' matches `int (&)(int, int)' with
A = {int, int} and is more specialized than `T &'.  The front end deduced
only the first element of a function parameter pack in a function type
written as a template argument and did not expand the pack in the pattern
of a pointer or reference to function, so `R (&)(A...)' compared unequal
and `T &' was selected (bin53: an invariant violation declaring a
variable of function type; `d<int (*)(int, int)>::arity' a conversion
error).  [dcl.ref]/1: `R (*)(B...)' must not match a reference to function
(the comparison ignored the reference attribute).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18
accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
